Combine component and charge constraints without discarding spectators (@chemistry-solution-charge: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../general-chemistry/aqueous/Acid-base-concepts-and-reactions.lingua#r_0EB1497NTKFZENB6Q1XQ3DQZGQ") { r_00FBD6BF76E407B3AFCCF72B70
A reaction conserves elements and electric charge. In a bulk aqueous region modeled as electrically neutral, the species concentrations also obey Σ_i z_i c_i=0, where z_i is each ion's signed charge number. This bulk approximation excludes charge-separated interfaces and transient charge accumulation; charge conservation alone does not say every arbitrarily small region is neutral. The model below concerns a well-mixed bulk liquid without an imposed electrical charge.

A component balance counts conserved chemical groups; a charge balance counts signed charge. They are independent constraints. Neutral HA contributes to the A-component balance but not the charge balance. A⁻ contributes one A group and charge number −1. Hydrated proton notation H⁺ contributes +1; OH⁻ contributes −1. In a solution with only these species and solvent, write C=[HA]+[A⁻] and [H⁺]=[OH⁻]+[A⁻]. The water solvent supplies or accepts protons, so [H⁺] is not automatically equal to the amount of acid originally introduced.

A simple neutralization account illustrates spectator cancellation. The ionic equation H₃O⁺+Cl⁻+Na⁺+OH⁻ → Na⁺+Cl⁻+2H₂O conserves H, O, Na, Cl and total charge. Cancelling unchanged Na⁺ and Cl⁻ gives H₃O⁺+OH⁻ → 2H₂O. Cancellation says nothing about their absence from the final solution. With additional acid HA, the bulk charge equation must include h+[Na⁺]=[OH⁻]+[A⁻]+[Cl⁻]. Equal sodium/chloride concentrations cancel from this equation but remain relevant to nonideal interactions. A sodium salt of A contributes a different counterion account from neutral HA at the same total A concentration.

For a weak acid, the selected proton-transfer reaction is HA+H₂O ⇌ H₃O⁺+A⁻. Treating HA as completely separated ions before applying its equilibrium relation assumes the very speciation we need to determine. The reversible equation identifies allowed change; the constant and conserved totals determine the equilibrium within the chosen model. Neither an equation's visual arrow nor a familiar name proves reaction completion.

**Read and work:** [Analytical Chemistry 2.1 — David Harvey](../general-chemistry/sources/Analytical-Chemistry-Harvey.lingua#r_0XMY75E3FRXRGR3BK28V3JAC0T), section 6.7's systematic-equilibrium and balance discussion, and the shared [Identify aqueous species and write a defensible net ionic equation](../general-chemistry/reactions/Aqueous-species-and-net-ionic-equations.lingua#r_1D7ZRSG6A9TD5G2879FVG4NS89) and [Choose an acid/base description that answers the actual question](../general-chemistry/aqueous/Acid-base-concepts-and-reactions.lingua#r_0EB1497NTKFZENB6Q1XQ3DQZGQ) material. Independently count charge and atoms in the neutralization account. Write balances for neutral HA and for its sodium salt and explain why they differ. Count the four unknown concentrations HA, A⁻, H⁺ and OH⁻; identify the two equilibrium relations still needed before solving them. Full redox, interface charging and electrochemistry retain later preparation.

**Continue:** [Choose an acid/base description that answers the actual question](../general-chemistry/aqueous/Acid-base-concepts-and-reactions.lingua#r_0EB1497NTKFZENB6Q1XQ3DQZGQ).

